WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.250
Welcome to Module 3 PostgreSQL in Action In this
module we are

00:00:05.262 --> 00:00:09.750
moving from designing a database to actually using
it In Module 2

00:00:09.912 --> 00:00:13.675
the main questions were about structure what tables
should exist which columns

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.788
belong in each table and how tables should relate to
one another

00:00:17.675 --> 00:00:22.275
Now our focus changes We will use SQL to ask
questions retrieve

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:26.812
useful rows and make controlled changes to stored
data If SQL is

00:00:26.838 --> 00:00:31.138
new to you do not worry about memorizing every
command I want

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:35.938
you to focus on a simple workflow First decide what
information you

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:40.862
need Second write the smallest SQL statement that
can answer that question

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:47.138
Third run the query Fourth inspect the rows changes
or error messages

00:00:47.162 --> 00:00:52.300
that PostgreSQL gives you Finally verify the result
before you move on

00:00:53.188 --> 00:00:57.750
Throughout this lesson you can run SQL directly in
the browser We

00:00:57.775 --> 00:01:02.612
will begin with complete examples Then you will make
small changes complete

00:01:02.638 --> 00:01:08.225
partially written statements and finally write short
queries yourself This gradual approach

00:01:08.238 --> 00:01:12.762
is intentional SQL becomes much easier when you
learn one pattern at

00:01:12.788 --> 00:01:17.325
a time As you work remember that reading data and
changing data

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:22.688
are different responsibilities A SELECT statement
normally reads data without changing the

00:01:22.712 --> 00:01:27.538
table Commands such as INSERT UPDATE and DELETE can
change stored data

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.950
so we will be more careful with them We will also
use

00:01:30.962 --> 00:01:36.362
transactions keys constraints and RETURNING to make
those changes safer Take your

00:01:36.388 --> 00:01:40.788
time with the examples Run them look at the result
and ask

00:01:40.812 --> 00:01:45.638
yourself what each clause contributed That habit is
more valuable than simply

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:46.988
memorizing syntax
